## Build and validate

Build, copy the produced DLL beside `plugin.wsgm.json`, then:

```powershell
dotnet build {{ROOT_NAMESPACE}}.csproj -c Release -r win-x64
wsgm-device validate <package-directory>
wsgm-device test plugin <package-directory> --from <inventory.json>
wsgm-device pack <package-directory> --out <plugin.wsgmpkg>
```

The hardware action is separate and always attended:

```powershell
wsgm-device test hardware <package-directory> --from <inventory.json> --state-dir <new-directory> --action haptic
```

Use `--action controller`, or
`--action capability --capability <id> --value <semantic-value> [--instance <id>]`, for the other
attended actions.

## Installing

Installing makes this package trusted hardware code that may later inherit WSGM's elevation, so
inspect the exact files before you do it.

Pack the plugin into a `.wsgmpkg`, close WSGM, and copy the file into the protected Plugins folder
from an elevated PowerShell. WSGM loads it from the file at the next start:

```powershell
Copy-Item -LiteralPath <plugin.wsgmpkg> -Destination (Join-Path $env:ProgramFiles 'WSGM\Plugins')
```

Only one device package may be installed; remove any other before starting WSGM.

For the complete workflow and the safety contract, see `docs/device-plugin-authoring.md` in the WSGM
repository.
